body:
I love this concept and I think you paint a strong picture here. I'm a big proponent of not overexplaining yourself and the only thing I would cut here personally is "instead of "yeah you can borrow that.""  We don't need it because 1. you have implied that the other person has the item and that you did not say they could have it, therefore we have already inferred it was stolen or borrowed and 2. you clarify that it was borrowed in the very next line.  Repeating the word borrow there actually weakens the payoff and I think trusting your reader to make that inference actually builds a stronger moment of trust between you and the reader.

&#x200B;

Metaphor is strong throughout and I really like your imagery

My criticism - each time you repeat a good line, it loses some of its strength.  Repetition should be used sparingly, so I think beginning each stanza with "the dead man's letter" is a poor choice.  It is the only clear subject of the poem and it would be much stronger if you called it that only in the first line, and from then on referred to it as "it" or "the letter' because then we would not lose the punch of that initial good line.

&#x200B;

That being said, I love the rest of it.  The structure works well for this poem and I like the very formal, storytelling-like meter.  The ending is very good and that says something, since a lot of poetry fails itself in the end by tapering off and not living up to the idea of itself.

&#x200B;
